---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
---
<BaseLayout title="Trevor Smith, Ph.D. | Emergent Robotics">
  <section class="candidate-hero shell">
    <div class="candidate-copy">
      <h1>Trevor Smith, <span>Ph.D.</span></h1>
      <p class="candidate-role"><strong>{profile.role}</strong><b>{profile.institution}</b><span>{profile.department}</span></p>
      <p class="candidate-credential"><span><b>2026</b> Ph.D., Mechanical Engineering · West Virginia University</span><i aria-hidden="true"></i><span>NSF Graduate Research Fellow</span></p>
      <div class="hero-vision"><h2><small>Emergent Robotics</small>Robots with adaptive behaviors <em>and</em> bodies.</h2><p>I study how collective intelligence, morphology, and behavior co-emerge through decentralized self-organization.</p></div>
      <div class="buttons"><a class="button" href="/research">Explore research</a><a class="button alt" href="/cv/Trevor_Smith_CV.pdf">Download CV ↓</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="candidate-photo"><img src="/images/trevor-smith.jpg" alt="Trevor Smith, Ph.D., robotics researcher"><div><strong>Emergent Robotics</strong></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="constraint problem-section shell"><div><h2><small>The problem</small>A fixed body will eventually fail.</h2><p class="lead">Too weak or damaged. Too large to enter or too small to act. A robot designed around a fixed body cannot survive in ever-changing real-world environments.</p></div><figure><video src="/changing_environments.mov" autoplay loop muted playsinline aria-label="A robot adapting to changing environments"></video><figcaption>Real-world environments change. A robot’s physical capabilities must change with them.</figcaption></figure></section>

  <section class="evolution" aria-labelledby="evolution-title"><div class="shell"><h2 id="evolution-title"><small>The historical trajectory</small>Intelligence became adaptive.<br>The body did not.</h2><div class="evolution-grid"><article><span>Past</span><h3>Fixed body<br>Fixed behavior</h3><p><strong>Industrial robotics.</strong> Robots repeat predefined motions in structured environments.</p></article><article><span>Present</span><h3>Fixed body<br>Adaptive behavior</h3><p><strong>Learning-enabled robotics.</strong> Modern learning changes how a robot acts, while its physical form remains fixed.</p></article><article><span>Future</span><h3>Adaptive body<br>Adaptive behavior</h3><p><strong>Emergent Robotics.</strong> My research extends adaptation into morphology: changing what a robot does and the body through which it acts.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="swarm-home"><div class="shell swarm-solution"><div><h2><small>The solution · Swarm of One</small>Building robots from self-organizing swarms.</h2><p>Inspired by multicellular organisms, many autonomous robotic cells coordinate through local interactions to create one coherent body, behavior, and intelligence. Their collective morphology can respond to environmental conditions rather than remaining fixed at design time.</p><a class="button" href="/research">Explore Swarm-of-One research →</a></div><img src="/images/loopy_light.png" alt="Loopy robotic cells forming a collective body"></div></section>

  <section class="progression shell"><h2><small>The evidence · Research grounded in the real-world</small>Each generation moved intelligence deeper into the machine’s components.</h2><p class="progression-intro">Field failures first motivated modularity. Modularity became distributed autonomy. Distributed autonomy became multicellular self-organization—and now a framework for 3D physical scaling.</p><div class="progression-line"><article><div class="progression-image"><a href="/research#urc" aria-label="View the Mars Rover Systems project"><img src="/images/research/urc.JPG" alt="Evolution of University Rover Challenge Mars rovers"></a><span>01</span></div><h3>URC Mars Rovers</h3><p>Field experience drove increasingly modular and distributed systems.</p></article><article><div class="progression-image"><a href="/research#stickbug" aria-label="View the Stickbug project"><img src="/images/research/stickbug.jpg" alt="Stickbug six-arm pollination robot"></a><span>02</span></div><h3>Stickbug</h3><p>Multiple autonomous sub-robots cooperate as one machine.</p></article><article><div class="progression-image"><a href="/research#loopy" aria-label="View the Loopy project"><img src="/images/research/loopy.jpg" alt="Loopy multicellular robot"></a><span>03</span></div><h3>Loopy</h3><p>A coherent robot emerges from 36 locally controlled robotic cells.</p></article><article><div class="progression-image"><a href="/research#polymorph" aria-label="View the Polymorph project"><img src="/images/research/polymorph.jpg" alt="Polymorph modular robotic cells"></a><span>04</span></div><h3>Polymorph</h3><p>A 3D cellular platform tests how Swarm-of-One robots scale physically.</p></article></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
